@module rooms
$B4B2 b StageGraphics 16 ; The stage's tiles, blocks and map, RLE-packed (3583 bytes; 4295 unpacked: 196 tiles, 114 blocks, the three grids' map)
> RoomChange $988B unpacks it with Unrle $A424 into the play buffer at $5B00 at every room change (the buffer is cleared again before the actors are drawn). Unrle: the first byte is the escape ($C7 here); a byte other than the escape is itself, ESC n v is n copies of v (n = 0: 256), ESC ESC ends. Unpacked: five words, the offsets from $5B0A of the tile kinds (1 byte a tile: 0 open, else solid; bit 1 solid, bit 3 deadly), the tile pixels (8 bytes a tile), the tile attributes (1 a tile), the blocks (4 tile numbers a block: top left, top right, bottom left, bottom right) and the map (a block number a byte, the grids one after another, each its width x height; Grid0 +6 gives its offset). RoomCells $9928 turns the room's 14 x 9 blocks into 28 x 18 tile numbers in Cells.
> The packer that made it is greedy: every run of 4 to 255 equal bytes is ESC n v, except one stretch of 8 zero bytes left as they are (unpacked offset 1366: a blank tile's pixels). With that one exception tools/res_stage.py packs the block again bit for bit, so the resource is editable: graphics.txt (the blocks and the map grids as text) and tiles.png (the tiles' pixels and colours). The same holds for the stages 2-4 (docs/img/maps/ has every grid and room). Read in all four stage-1 builds, every byte (Unrle).
@res stage1/graphics.bin

@module graphics ; The stage's sprites, pictures and their frame tables
@about Every animated actor shows frames of a sprite set: the bitmaps, then a frame table of 6-byte records (dx, dy, the width in bytes, the height in lines, the bitmap's offset from the set's start). SetActorFrame $AA02 takes the frame number in A and the table's and the set's addresses from the four bytes after its CALL, and fills the actor's +0/+1 (+$0D + dx, negated when the actor faces left, and +$0E + dy), +2/+3 and +4/+5. A bitmap is 1 bit a pixel, the rows top first, no mask; DrawActorSprite $A6AA ORs it into the play buffer through the row plotters (RowPlotters $8910), mirrored through the resident bit-reversal table $FA00 when the actor faces left; DrawSprite $B42B copies a picture with LDIR. Each set's bitmaps are exactly the bytes its frames cover, and its table follows them: so each set is one resource (sprites-NAME.png with the table in its text chunk). The plain pictures (shots, icons, the boss's parts) are bitmaps-NAME-WxH.png, frames of one size one after another.
@about The sets: the hero's walk, jump, leap and strikes; the urns and chests; the pieces of the artefact; the warriors (stage 1: skeletons) and their collapse; the statue heads; the hanging bodies; the flame jets and fire pits; the gate and the bridge slab; the dragon's head with its body picture and the winged demon's wings, body and head with the face over its lair. What no stage-1 recording reads (the demon, the dragon, the shots, the bridge, the fire pit) is named from the code that reads it.
$C2B1 b BridgeSlabSprite 4 ; The bridge slab: 4 bytes x 8 lines (BridgeSlabTemplate's frame; the demon's beaten wing lays it, BridgeSlabActor $90A3)
> Not read in the stage-1 recordings (none reaches the demon); known only from the code ($9D27, the template's +4).
